# Source spreadsheets for the founding-member import: personal data (names,
# phones, e-mails, Telegram ids, addresses, birth dates). The migration carries
# the derived subset it needs in backend/src/migrations/seed-assets/; keeping the
# raw workbooks and the intermediate dataset out of git limits that to one copy.
# Regenerate with scripts/member-import/{build_dataset,generate_migration,
# build_events,generate_events_migration}.py
docs/members/*.xlsx
scripts/member-import/dataset.json
# Attendance by register name — who attended which event, same PII class.
scripts/member-import/events.json
# The Telegram channel export: 98 announcements plus ~11 MB of images, of which
# the 79 an event actually points at are committed under
# backend/src/migrations/seed-assets/event-covers/. Keeping the raw export out
# avoids carrying those bytes twice, and matches the rule above for the source
# workbooks. Regenerate with build_event_details.py.
docs/events/
scripts/member-import/event_details.json
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Local pg_dump output from scripts/reset-data.sh — plain-text production PII.
backups/

# Member photos exported from Drive for the founding-member import.
docs/members/photos/
docs/db-dump/

# The 2026-09 client drop of the same questionnaire in CSV form, plus the merged
# table generated from it. Same PII class as the workbooks above — full
# questionnaire responses with phones, e-mails, addresses and birth dates — and
# the same rule applies: the derived subset lives in seed-assets/, the raw source
# does not enter git. Regenerate the merged table with
# scripts/member-import/merge_new_input.py.
docs/members/New-input/

# pg_dump output left in the repo root (`ua-well-prod-…-dump.sql`). Plain-text
# production PII for the whole database, not just the members.
/*-dump.sql
